WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
You already know React, so building a mobile app should feel intuitive,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
but applying web techniques to a phone means components don't behave as expected.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Web developers have been told for years that they can learn once,

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
write anywhere with React.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
While React Native uses the React library,

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
it swaps the browser's standard APIs for a native mobile environment.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
This split-screen comparison highlights the divergence.

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
The browser sandbox handles different tasks than the Hermes engine.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
In a browser, you rely on the document object model,

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
a CSS engine, and a URL bar to manage state and navigation.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The Hermes engine removes these tools.

12
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
There is no global document window or URL-based routing.

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Instead, the engine prioritizes touch gestures and direct storage access.

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Relying on browser-specific tools leads to broken layouts and missing data

15
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
that keep an app from being shipable.

16
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The transition starts with the absence of HTML.

17
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Standard div or span tags are not supported in React Native,

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
including them causes the app to crash immediately.

19
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
You replace web markup with mobile-specific primitives.

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
You build your interface using foundational blocks like View and Text.

21
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Styling also moves away from a global cascading system.

22
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Each component receives its own styles defined as independent JavaScript objects.

23
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Core React Logic remains the same.

24
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.000
You still use state, hooks, and the fetch API to manage your application data.

25
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
The way components update and handle data flow is familiar,

26
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.000
even though the rendering engine is different.

27
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Success in mobile development requires thinking in terms of these isolated UI primitives,

28
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
rather than a linked document.

29
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Mobile apps also operate without a URL bar.

30
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Mobile operating systems do not use URL changes to switch between views.

31
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Instead, you use navigation patterns,

32
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
where screens are stacked on top of each other or organized into tabs.

33
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Standard browser persistence like local storage and session storage are unavailable here.

34
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Saving user data requires integrating asynchronous storage libraries like MMKV to handle persistence.

35
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Navigation and memory are structures you must actively build and manage

36
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
as part of the app's internal state.

37
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Mobile development also means accounting for physical hardware.

38
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Screen real estate is interrupted by camera notches and home indicators.

39
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Without proper adjustments, the OS renders UI elements directly behind these physical obstructions,

40
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
making buttons impossible to press.

41
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
You prevent this by defining safe areas for your content.

42
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Using safe area context ensures your layout remains clear of the edges,

43
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.000
effectively shrinking the usable area to fit within the visible screen.

44
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Software also changes the layout in real time,

45
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
specifically when the virtual keyboard appears.

46
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
When the keyboard slides up, it often obscures the input field the user is currently typing in.

47
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
You solve this by wrapping forms in a keyboard avoiding view,

48
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
which detects the keyboard and responds by adjusting the view height.

49
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
A mobile UI constantly changes based on the physical device and active software overlays.

50
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.000
User interaction shifts from high precision clicking to tactile touch,

51
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.000
treating a mobile screen like a mouse driven interface results in an app that feels unresponsive and difficult to use.

52
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Modern users expect intuitive gestures like fluid sideways swipes and deliberate long presses

53
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
to effectively navigate and interact with the mobile app.

54
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Development requires adding gesture handlers to interpret these complex pressure sensitive inputs.

55
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.000
To create a native field, you must replace cursor logic with systems designed for thumbs and momentum.

56
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Moving to mobile is an exercise in working within the specific physical constraints of handheld hardware.

57
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
This chart shows the expected learning curve based on your existing web development strengths.

58
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
If you specialize in application logic and state management, many of your skills port over easily,

59
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.000
leading to a faster transition.

60
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
If your work is primarily UI-driven and relies on CSS cascades, you will face a significant adjustment,

61
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.000
as you learn native layout rules.

62
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Modern tools like Expo and pre-configured TypeScript templates handle the infrastructure setup,

63
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
allowing you to focus on these environment differences.

64
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Building for mobile requires actively working with the physical devices constraints

65
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
in order to create truly responsive native experiences.

